            introduction: "In this lesson you will learn jobs, steps and runners. Jobs are the units of work in a workflow, steps are the commands inside a job, and runners are the machines that execute them.",
            concept: "Jobs are the units of work in a workflow, steps are the commands inside a job, and runners are the machines that execute them. Jobs run in parallel by default, and needs: [build] makes one job wait for another. A step can run a shell command or use an action, and steps share the same runner filesystem, so a build step's output is visible to a later test step. GitHub-hosted runners are fresh virtual machines for every job — nothing persists between runs — while self-hosted runners live on your own machines and suit private networks or special hardware. Each step's exit code decides whether the job continues: a failing step stops the job unless continue-on-error is set.",
            syntax: "jobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm ci && npm run build\n  test:\n    needs: build\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest",
            analogy: "Jobs are like stations on an assembly line: each one does its task, and the next station only starts when the previous one hands over a finished part.",
            exampleCode: "needs: build",
