            introduction: "In this lesson you will learn dependency caching. Caching saves the dependency folder between runs, turning a two-minute install into a ten-second restore.",
            concept: "Caching saves the dependency folder between runs, turning a two-minute install into a ten-second restore. actions/cache restores node_modules (or the npm cache) keyed by a hash of package-lock.json, so the cache is used only when dependencies have not changed. setup-node has a built-in cache: 'npm' option that does the same with one line. A cache hit skips the install step, and a miss installs and saves the new cache. Caches are per-branch, scoped to the repository, and evicted after seven days of disuse, so a cache is a speed-up, never a guarantee.",
            syntax: "steps:\n  - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n    with:\n      node-version: '20'\n      cache: 'npm'",
            analogy: "Caching is like keeping leftovers in the fridge: tonight's dinner is ready in minutes, but you still cook fresh when the ingredients change.",
