            introduction: "In this lesson you will learn environments, approvals and rollbacks. Environments add a gate between a workflow and production: a named environment can require a human approval before anything deploys.",
            concept: "Environments add a gate between a workflow and production: a named environment can require a human approval before anything deploys. You declare environment: production on the deploy job, and GitHub pauses the run until an approved reviewer clicks Approve. Environment secrets are separate from repository secrets, so production credentials are only visible to production deploys. If a release misbehaves, the rollback is a redeploy of the previous known-good version — which is why every deploy should record which commit it shipped. A failed deploy that cannot be rolled back in minutes is a design flaw, not bad luck.",
            syntax: "jobs:\n  deploy:\n    environment: production\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest",
